@startuml scoped-alert-delivery
title US-15: Scoped Alert Delivery - Server Filters Before Pushing

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

participant "Alert\nEvaluator\n(leader)" as Evaluator
queue "Kafka\nalerts" as Kafka
participant "Express API\n(WebSocket server)" as API
database "Redis\nentity:live:{id}" as Redis
participant "Operator A\n(scope: France,\naircraft+vessel,\nSIGNAL_LOSS)" as OpA
participant "Operator B\n(scope: Atlantic,\nvessel only,\nall alert types)" as OpB

Evaluator -> Kafka : publish alert event\n{ alert_id: FLT123:SIGNAL_LOSS:1700010000000,\n  entity_id: FLT123,\n  entity_type: aircraft,\n  alert_type: SIGNAL_LOSS,\n  lat: 48.8, lon: 2.3 }

Kafka -> API : consume alert event

note over API
  API maintains in-memory scope map for
  all active WebSocket connections:
  { conn_A -> scope_A, conn_B -> scope_B }
  Fan-out evaluates each scope independently.
end note

API -> Redis : HGETALL entity:live:FLT123

note over Redis
  Redis holds the entity's last known position.
  Used to evaluate the geo_region bounds check.
  If the entity is dark (key expired), last known
  position from the alert payload is used instead.
end note

Redis --> API : { lat: 48.8, lon: 2.3,\n  entity_type: aircraft,\n  timestamp_ms: ... }

== Scope evaluation for Operator A ==

note over API
  Operator A scope check:
  geo: lat 48.8, lon 2.3 within France bounds? YES
  entity_type: aircraft in [aircraft, vessel]? YES
  alert_type: SIGNAL_LOSS in [SIGNAL_LOSS]? YES
  -> PUSH
end note

API -> OpA : WebSocket push\n{ alert_id, entity_id, alert_type,\n  lat, lon, summary }

OpA --> OpA : alert appears on map\nand in alert panel

== Scope evaluation for Operator B ==

note over API
  Operator B scope check:
  geo: lat 48.8, lon 2.3 within Atlantic bounds? NO
  -> DROP (no further checks needed)
end note

note over OpB
  Operator B receives nothing.
  FLT123 is over France, not the Atlantic.
  Client-side filtering would have sent this
  alert to Operator B and let the browser discard it.
  Server-side filtering prevents that entirely.
end note

@enduml
